      "agent_output": {
        "role": "You are the common-knowledge and architecture specialist. \nYou have no tools. Answer stable questions from your knowled. \nYou also know this workflow architecture: a routing tool-use agent chooses WebSearch, Summary, or CommonKnowledge; WebSearch can call web_search repeatedly; Summary synthesizes the collected web context; and the final specialist answer is returned to the user.\nExplain that architecture when the user asks about it.\nDo not pretend to have performed web research.",
        "response": "TCP and UDP are two core transport-layer protocols in the Internet protocol suite (usually over IP). They differ mainly in reliability, ordering, and overhead.\n\n**TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)**\n- **Connection-oriented:** Establishes a connection (handshake) before data is sent.\n- **Reliable delivery:** Uses acknowledgments and retransmissions to recover lost packets.\n- **In-order delivery:** Ensures the receiving application gets data in the order sent.\n- **Flow/congestion control:** Includes mechanisms to avoid overwhelming the network or the receiver.\n- **Overhead:** More control information (handshake, ACKs, retransmissions), so typically more latency and complexity.\n- **Common uses:** HTTP/HTTPS (most web traffic), email (SMTP over TCP), file transfer (FTP), SSH.\n\n**UDP (User Datagram Protocol)**\n- **Connectionless:** No handshake; each packet is sent independently.\n- **Unreliable delivery:** No acknowledgments or retransmissions built in.\n- **No ordering guarantees:** Packets may arrive out of order, be dropped, or be duplicated.\n- **Lower overhead:** Minimal protocol logic, generally lower latency.\n- **Common uses:** DNS (often), streaming/real-time media (RTP/VoIP), gaming, VoD, custom protocols where the app handles reliability.\n\n**Rule of thumb**\n- Choose **TCP** when you need **correctness** and **ordered, reliable** delivery.\n- Choose **UDP** when you need **speed/low latency** and can tolerate occasional loss or can implement reliability at the application layer.\n\nIf you tell me your use case (e.g., video chat, web app, game), I can suggest which fits best and why.",
